<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marxism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848 CE</a> – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marxism is a materialist philosophy and method of analysis that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> developed in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, centred on class struggle and the critique of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It does not adopt <b>Marxism</b> wholesale, but its use of concepts such as class struggle and its critique of global capitalism led to significant controversy within the Church.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, leaders of the movement, were the most influential promoters of <b>Marxism</b> in China in these years and became founders of the Chinese Communist Party in 1921; others drawn to communist ideas included Chen Tanqiu and Zhou Enlai.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Senghor claimed that &quot;Africa's social background of tribal community life not only makes socialism natural to Africa but excludes the validity of the theory of class struggle&quot;, which made African socialism, in all its variations, different from <b>Marxism</b> and European socialist theory.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/african-socialism">African socialism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars dispute whether it is <b>Marxism</b>, nationalism or a quasi-religion.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/juche">Juche · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Others reject such a link: Robert Vincent Daniels held that <b>Marxism</b> only justified Stalinism, Graeme Gill called Stalinism a sharp break from earlier developments, and Michael Geyer and Sheila Fitzpatrick criticised the totalitarian model for obscuring how the systems worked.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Bolshevik teaching viewed Pan-Slavism as a reactionary element associated with the Russian Empire and contrary to <b>Marxism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-slavism">Pan-Slavism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
